 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for committed-change delivery.** Five-second HTTPS polling would use simpler stateless request/response delivery but add repeated reads and up to several seconds of update lag. A hosted realtime service would reduce connection-management work but add recurring fees and provider coupling. Self-hosted WebSockets provide low-latency push without that vendor dependency; the project accepts responsibility for connection capacity and reconnect behavior. This choice requires replayable revisions and reliable publication in the change store, session management in delivery, and reconnect/catch-up in browser sync. See [the delivery decision record](decisions/self-hosted-websocket-delivery.md) for the full trade-off analysis.
